    "ogHeadline": "ANIME ON SNEAKERS:\nWHAT ACTUALLY SOLD",
    "ogAccent": "F47521",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Adidas_Store_SoHo.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Kidfly182 / Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adidas_Store_SoHo.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "trendScore": 58,
    "body": "The anime sneaker collab is now a standing category with its own release calendar, and almost all of it descends from one 2018 project. adidas Originals announced a *Dragon Ball Z* collection that paired silhouettes to characters and rolled them out across a season instead of dropping them at once. That structure — character-per-shoe, staggered releases, an arc rather than a pack — is the template every anime collab since has either copied or failed by ignoring.\n\n## adidas x Dragon Ball Z set the format\n\nadidas [announced the collection](https://news.adidas.com/originals/adidas-originals-announce-dragon-ball-z-collaboration/s/29914a39-9660-4eba-931c-8f0f962b6735) through its own newsroom rather than a leak, which tells you how much weight the brand put on it. Seven silhouettes, seven characters, matched pairings: Goku against Frieza, Gohan against Cell, and so on, released as rivalries rather than as a single collection drop.\n\nThe design logic held up because it respected both sides. adidas did not reskin a runner in orange and call it Goku. The pairings used existing Originals models with material choices that tracked the character design, and the rival-pair release schedule gave collectors a reason to care about pair two after pair one.\n\nWhat it proved commercially: anime IP could move a general-release sneaker at retail price, at scale, without a rapper attached. That was not obvious in 2018. It has been the assumption ever since.\n\n## The Naruto pipeline\n\n*Naruto* was the next obvious property, and the path there was messier. [Complex](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/riley-jones/naruto-adidas-collaboration-release-date) and [Highsnobiety](https://www.highsnobiety.com/p/naruto-adidas-collection-release-date-price/) both tracked the adidas x *Naruto* collection as the follow-up to *Dragon Ball Z*, working through the same character-pairing logic with a franchise whose fandom skews younger and buys more heavily into apparel.\n\nThen Converse took the property downmarket and made the smartest move in the category. The [*Naruto Shippuden* x Converse Chuck Taylor collection](https://sneakernews.com/2025/04/15/naruto-converse-chuck-taylor-release-date/) put five characters on the cheapest, most-printed silhouette in the industry — a canvas shoe that has been a blank for graphic treatments for sixty years. [Hypebeast](https://hypebeast.com/2025/5/naruto-shippuden-converse-chuck-taylor-pack-naruto-uzumaki-sasuke-uchiha-gaara-a14836c-a14837c-a14838c-release-info) ran the official looks ahead of the May 2025 release.\n\nThe Chuck is the correct canvas for this, literally. Anime collabs fail when they put a busy character graphic on a technical runner with no room for it. They work when the shoe is already a billboard.\n\n## What actually moved, and what didn't\n\nStrip the hype and the pattern is consistent across the category, which [SNKRDUNK](https://snkrdunk.com/en/magazine/2022/09/01/2022-best-anime-sneaker-collabs-so-far/) and [Sole Retriever](https://www.soleretriever.com/news/articles/best-anime-sneakers-of-all-time-naruto-dragon-ball-z-one-piece-2024) have both documented across years of releases:\n\nThe ones that sell are built on a silhouette people already wanted, use restraint on the graphic, and tie to a franchise with a live broadcast presence rather than a dormant one. The ones that sit are the opposite: a forgettable model, a full-panel character print, and a property whose audience aged out five years ago. Storytelling through the release schedule beats storytelling on the upper.\n\n## Who is getting paid\n\nThis is a licensing business, and the license flows one direction. The Japanese rights holder — Shueisha, Toei, the production committee — collects on every unit. The footwear brand books the margin and the marketing halo. The retail partner takes its cut.\n\nThe audience that made anime a viable sneaker property in the first place — the Toonami generation, the Black collectors who put anime graphics on custom Air Force 1s and Vans long before any brand licensed a character — is in the model exclusively as demand. There is no anime collab in the category designed by a Black artist with a royalty position on the shoe, which is notable given that the same brands have no trouble structuring exactly that deal when the collaborator is a musician.\n\n## The take\n\nAnime collabs are the cleanest example of how a brand monetizes taste it did not create. Kids drew Super Saiyan hair on canvas shoes for twenty years for free; adidas made it a licensed line in 2018 and the category has been profitable ever since. The fix is not to stop buying them. It is to notice that the missing name on every one of these boxes is a Black designer with points on the back end, and to keep asking why a category built on the Toonami audience has never once been handed to one.\n\n## Sources\n- [adidas Originals announce Dragon Ball Z collaboration](https://news.adidas.com/originals/adidas-originals-announce-dragon-ball-z-collaboration/s/29914a39-9660-4eba-931c-8f0f962b6735) — adidas News\n- [adidas Has a Sneaker Collab With 'Naruto'](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/riley-jones/naruto-adidas-collaboration-release-date) — Complex\n- [Five Characters Appear In The Naruto Shippuden x Converse Collection](https://sneakernews.com/2025/04/15/naruto-converse-chuck-taylor-release-date/) — Sneaker News, April 15, 2025\n- [Official Look at the 'Naruto Shippuden' x Converse Collaboration](https://hypebeast.com/2025/5/naruto-shippuden-converse-chuck-taylor-pack-naruto-uzumaki-sasuke-uchiha-gaara-a14836c-a14837c-a14838c-release-info) — Hypebeast, May 2025\n- [Best Anime Sneakers of All Time](https://www.soleretriever.com/news/articles/best-anime-sneakers-of-all-time-naruto-dragon-ball-z-one-piece-2024) — Sole Retriever\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-30",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-09-30"
