    "body": "Chipmunk soul was a production style and a business model, and Roc-A-Fella ran both from a single Manhattan office. Take a 1970s soul vocal, pitch it up until the singer sounds like a child, chop it against hard drums, and give it to Jay-Z. Kanye West and Just Blaze perfected it on *The Blueprint*, reportedly cut in two weeks with Jay recording nine songs in two days, per [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blueprint). The album sold 427,000 in its first week, held No. 1 for three weeks, and in 2018 became the first 21st-century recording added to the National Recording Registry. Every loop on it belonged to somebody before it belonged to the Roc.\n\n## The ledger\n\nWikipedia and [DJBooth's](https://djbooth.net/features/2019-01-22-25-greatest-chipmunk-soul-beats-roc-a-fella/) 2019 rundown of the era agree on the credits. Kanye produced four tracks: \"Takeover,\" \"Izzo (H.O.V.A.),\" \"Heart of the City (Ain't No Love)\" and \"Never Change.\" Just Blaze produced four: \"Girls, Girls, Girls,\" \"U Don't Know,\" \"Song Cry\" and the hidden \"Breathe Easy (Lyrical Exercise).\" Bink produced three, including \"The Ruler's Back.\" Timbaland did \"Hola' Hovito,\" and Eminem produced \"Renegade.\"\n\nNow the sources. \"Izzo\" is the Jackson 5's \"I Want You Back.\" \"Heart of the City\" is Bobby \"Blue\" Bland's 1974 \"Ain't No Love in the Heart of the City.\" \"Never Change\" is David Ruffin's 1973 \"Common Man.\" \"Girls, Girls, Girls\" is Tom Brock's 1974 \"There's Nothing in This World That Can Stop Me From Loving You.\" \"U Don't Know\" is Bobby Byrd's 1970 \"I'm Not to Blame.\" \"Song Cry\" is Bobby Glenn's 1976 \"Sounds Like a Love Song.\" Six soul records, six Black artists, and six sets of rights holders, of whom Motown, Duke/Peacock's successors and various independents controlled the masters and publishers controlled the songs.\n\n## What \"cleared\" meant in 2001\n\nTen years after Grand Upright, no major label released an uncleared sample on a flagship album. Roc-A-Fella, distributed by Def Jam, cleared everything on *The Blueprint*, which is why the album has never been pulled or re-edited. That is a cost, and the cost is invisible to the listener. A sample clearance on a record this size typically means a master fee to the label and a publishing share to the songwriters of the original, which reduces the writing share of the new song. Jay-Z, Kanye and Just Blaze wrote *The Blueprint*, but Berry Gordy's Jobete, the Jackson 5's writers, and Bobby Bland's publisher all own pieces of it in perpetuity.\n\nThe chipmunk technique was, in that sense, a negotiating tool. Pitching the vocal up made the sample less recognizable to a casual listener and shorter in real time, since a sped-up phrase takes fewer seconds. Under Bridgeport, which arrived four years later, that would not have mattered legally. Under the industry's own risk math in 2001, it lowered the price.\n\n## Just Blaze versus Kanye\n\nThe two producers split the era stylistically. Just Blaze cut with drums that hit like a live kit and let the sample breathe over eight bars; \"U Don't Know\" is a Bobby Byrd horn line played almost straight, at DJBooth's No. 3. Kanye chopped harder and pitched higher; \"Izzo\" turns the Jackson 5 into a hook rather than a bed and sits at No. 4. Both approaches ran through Roc-A-Fella's roster: Just Blaze's \"What We Do\" for Freeway, Kanye's \"This Can't Be Life\" and \"Nothing Like It\" for Beanie Sigel, and Kanye's own \"Through the Wire,\" DJBooth's No. 1, built on Chaka Khan's \"Through the Fire\" and recorded with his jaw wired shut.\n\nJust Blaze never got his own solo album; Kanye's *The College Dropout* in 2004 turned the style into a solo career and, eventually, into a fashion company. That divergence is the era's real story. Same technique, same office, two very different outcomes for the men who invented it.\n\n## The take\n\nChipmunk soul is a Black producer paying a Black singer's estate and label for the right to make a Black rapper's record, and everybody in that chain except the producer had leverage. The singers, or more often their labels, got fees and a slice of the publishing forever. Roc-A-Fella, co-owned by Jay-Z, Dame Dash and Kareem Burke, owned the masters, which is why the Roc's equity later became a real fight. Kanye and Just Blaze got production fees, points, and a reputation.\n\nWhat the era proved is that the sample is the asset and the asset always has an owner. The Roc understood that and bought in. The next generation, from Metro Boomin to Southside, took the lesson further and stopped sampling at all. The chipmunk voice was a workaround. Owning the room is the fix.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [The Blueprint](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blueprint) — Wikipedia, accessed September 2026\n- [25 Greatest Chipmunk Soul Beats from the Roc-A-Fella Dynasty](https://djbooth.net/features/2019-01-22-25-greatest-chipmunk-soul-beats-roc-a-fella/) — DJBooth, January 22, 2019\n- [\"All Samples Cleared!\" Remembering Biz Markie's Contributions to Copyright Law](https://www.cll.com/CopyrightDevelopmentsBlog/all-samples-cleared-remembering-biz-markies-contributions) — Cowan, Liebowitz & Latman, August 17, 2021\n- [Bridgeport Music, Inc. v. Dimension Films](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgeport_Music,_Inc._v._Dimension_Films) — Wikipedia, accessed September 2026\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-07",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-10-07"
