    "slug": "bad-bunny-adidas-forum-buckle-gazelle-campus-signature-run",
    "pillar": "sneakers",
    "title": "Bad Bunny Became adidas's Biggest Signature Name",
    "headline": "Bad Bunny Became adidas's\nBiggest Signature Name",
    "subhead": "From the Forum Buckle Low to the Gazelle and Campus runs, a Puerto Rican artist quietly took over the job adidas had spent a decade outsourcing.",
    "description": "Forum Buckle Low to Gazelle and Campus: how Bad Bunny became adidas Originals' most reliable signature partner after the Yeezy collapse.",
    "keywords": [
      "Bad Bunny",
      "adidas Originals",
      "Forum Buckle Low",
      "adidas Campus",
      "adidas Gazelle",
      "Response CL",
      "Benito Martinez",
      "Puerto Rico",
      "signature sneaker",
      "Ballerina",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "BAD BUNNY BECAME ADIDAS'S\nBIGGEST SIGNATURE NAME",
    "ogAccent": "F26A1B",
    "heroImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Bad_Bunny_at_Forbes_Under_30_2023.jpg",
    "heroCredit": "Wikimedia Commons",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad_Bunny_at_Forbes_Under_30_2023.jpg",
    "heroFocus": "center top",
    "trendScore": 59,
    "body": "adidas spent the 2010s betting its cultural relevance on one partnership and then watched it detonate. The recovery did not come from a new mega-deal. It came from a Puerto Rican artist who had been quietly shipping sold-out shoes the whole time, on silhouettes so ordinary that nobody outside the market noticed they were running the table.\n\n## The Forum was the proof of concept\n\nThe partnership started on the Forum — a 1984 basketball shoe adidas had been trying to revive for years without success. Benito's versions swapped the ankle strap for a buckle, ran in cream and off-white palettes, and added small Puerto Rican flag details. They sold out instantly and kept selling out. [Sneaker Freaker's history of the collaborations](https://www.sneakerfreaker.com/features/a-comprehensive-history-of-bad-bunny-s-sneaker-collaborations) tracks the run from the first Forum Powerphase through the Buckle Lows and the Ballerina, and the pattern holds across every release: modest silhouette, tight color story, immediate sellout.\n\nThat is a fundamentally different model from the one adidas had been running. There was no separate brand, no standalone imprint, no distribution built outside adidas's own channels. The shoes went out as adidas Originals product with an artist's fingerprints on them.\n\n## Then the terrace shoes took over the world\n\nThe Gazelle and the Campus did not need Bad Bunny to become the dominant silhouettes of the mid-2020s — that was a broader swing back toward low-profile suede after a decade of chunky runners. But his versions gave adidas the cultural anchor for the moment. [adidas's own collaboration timeline](https://www.adidas.com/us/blog/bad-bunny-archive-the-complete-adidas-collaboration-timeline) lays out how quickly the releases stacked up once the terrace models took over, and [Complex ranked the collabs](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/zac-dubasik/bad-bunny-adidas-collab-ranking) as a body of work rather than a series of one-offs.\n\n[Billboard has noted that his adidas releases are reliably best-sellers](https://www.billboard.com/culture/product-recommendations/bad-bunny-adidas-new-badbo-1-0-sneaker-shop-online-1236208577/), which is the number that actually matters to a company that needed proof it could still move product on a name.\n\nThe Response CL deserves its own note. A dull early-2000s runner nobody had thought about in fifteen years came back in his colorways and moved like a limited release, which told adidas something valuable about its own archive: the constraint was never the shoe, it was the storyteller attached to it. That insight has driven most of the brand's product planning since.\n\n## The economics of being the replacement\n\nHere is the part that deserves more argument than it gets. adidas's previous signature partnership was structured around a separate brand with its own royalty stream and its own creative control, and when it ended, the company took a nine-figure hit and spent two years liquidating inventory. The Bad Bunny arrangement looks deliberately safer from adidas's side: fewer standalone assets, more integration into existing Originals lines, less exposure if the relationship ends.\n\nSafer for adidas also means less transferable for the artist. A signature line that lives inside the company's own silhouettes does not become an independent business the way a separately branded imprint can. The upside is stability; the cost is that when it ends, the artist walks away with a résumé rather than a company.\n\nFor a Latin artist selling out stadiums and headlining the Super Bowl conversation, that is a real question about leverage — and it is the same question Black athletes and artists have been navigating with Nike, Reebok and adidas since the 1980s. [Stadium Goods' history of the partnership](https://www.stadiumgoods.com/blogs/news/a-history-of-bad-bunny-x-adidas) reads like a catalog of wins that all belong to the same corporate ledger.\n\n## The take\n\nThe most important thing Bad Bunny did for adidas was prove that the company could still make a hit without handing over the keys. That is a good outcome for a German shoe corporation and a more complicated one for the culture, because the model it validates is the one where the artist supplies the audience and the brand keeps the asset. The Forum Buckle Low will be remembered as a great shoe. Whether it built anything Benito owns is a different question, and the honest answer right now is that the Puerto Rican flag on the heel is worth more to adidas's balance sheet than the deal is worth to his.\n\n## Sources\n- [A Comprehensive History of Bad Bunny's Sneaker Collaborations](https://www.sneakerfreaker.com/features/a-comprehensive-history-of-bad-bunny-s-sneaker-collaborations) — Sneaker Freaker\n- [Bad Bunny Archive: The Complete adidas Collaboration Timeline](https://www.adidas.com/us/blog/bad-bunny-archive-the-complete-adidas-collaboration-timeline) — adidas\n- [Bad Bunny's Best Adidas Sneaker Collabs, Ranked](https://www.complex.com/sneakers/a/zac-dubasik/bad-bunny-adidas-collab-ranking) — Complex\n- [Bad Bunny's Adidas Collaborations are Always a Best-Seller](https://www.billboard.com/culture/product-recommendations/bad-bunny-adidas-new-badbo-1-0-sneaker-shop-online-1236208577/) — Billboard\n- [A History of Bad Bunny x adidas](https://www.stadiumgoods.com/blogs/news/a-history-of-bad-bunny-x-adidas) — Stadium Goods\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-12",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-10-12"
