    "body": "A black cloak with red clouds on it is now as readable on a rap stage as a chain or a varsity jacket. That is a strange outcome for the uniform of a fictional terrorist organization from a Japanese comic about ninja orphans, and it did not happen because rappers like cartoons. It happened because *Naruto* hands an artist a complete symbolic system — crews, defectors, bloodlines, a village that raised you and then feared you — and rap has always run on exactly that.\n\n## The show gives you a vocabulary, not a reference\n\nAnime references in rap are usually described as fan service. The functional truth is closer to sampling: a rapper invokes Itachi or the Sharingan for the same reason a producer flips a familiar break — the audience already knows the emotional information, so the bar arrives pre-loaded.\n\n*Naruto* is unusually well suited to it. The protagonist is an orphan the village shuns, carrying something dangerous inside him that everyone can sense; he gets strong in public, and the same people who wrote him off want in. Wikipedia's entry on [anime and hip-hop](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anime_and_hip-hop) tracks how thoroughly that structure has been absorbed on the rap side — not as trivia, but as a shared narrative grammar between two audiences who were often the same audience.\n\nThe Akatsuki, specifically, are the crew story: a group of the most talented defectors alive, each one an S-class problem, operating outside every village that trained them. Anyone who has watched a regional rap scene lose its best artists to the majors understands the appeal of that image without needing the plot explained.\n\n## From bars to fits\n\nThe wardrobe followed the writing. [Revolt](https://www.revolt.tv/article/lil-uzi-vert-best-blerd-moments-from-anime-to-babymetal) has documented Lil Uzi Vert's long run of anime-forward moments — the reference points in the music, the styling, the willingness to show up looking like a character rather than a rapper dressed as one. That permission structure matters more than any single outfit. Once one major artist treats anime as wardrobe rather than as a hobby to keep quiet about, the whole field opens.\n\n[HotNewHipHop](https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/674528-rappers-like-anime) has catalogued the broader roster of artists who build openly around anime, and the pattern holds across regions and eras: it is not a subgenre marker, it is closer to universal among artists who came up after Toonami put the shows in every house with basic cable.\n\nMegan Thee Stallion is the clearest case of that going all the way through — cosplay at the [Crunchyroll Anime Awards](https://www.complex.com/music/a/alex-ocho/megan-thee-stallion-stuns-in-cosplay-for-crunchyroll) in Tokyo, anime-built visuals, and a track on her 2024 album called \"Otaku Hot Girl.\" That is a rapper treating the culture as identity rather than as a costume for a press cycle.\n\n## Who sells the cloak\n\nHere is the part the fan pieces skip. That cloak is licensed merchandise, and the licensing runs through the Japanese rights holders and their Western distribution partners. Every time a rapper makes an Akatsuki fit look expensive, somebody with no relationship to that rapper books the sell-through. Anime licensing to streetwear and footwear has become a dependable revenue line precisely because Black artists made those images fashionable on stages and in videos, at no charge, for twenty years.\n\nThe artists who convert that attention into their own product — their own merch line, their own animation project, their own IP — are the exception rather than the rule. The default is free promotion for a catalog somebody else owns, which should sound familiar to anybody who has watched a regional sound get harvested by a major label.\n\n## The take\n\nThe cloak is not a costume, it's a claim: I am from a place that made me and then wanted no part of me, and I found my people outside it. Rap has been telling that story since it started. *Naruto* just gave it a silhouette that reads at 60 feet from a festival stage. The aesthetic belongs to the audience that adopted it. The revenue does not. Until Black artists are building anime properties they own rather than wearing ones they don't — and [some are trying](https://sneakzandbeatz.com/the-lane/michael-b-jordan-anime-projects-in-development-vogue-quit-it-all-2026) — that gap stays exactly where it is.\n\n## Sources\n- [Anime and hip-hop](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anime_and_hip-hop) — Wikipedia, accessed 2026\n- [Lil Uzi Vert's best blerd moments, from anime to BABYMETAL](https://www.revolt.tv/article/lil-uzi-vert-best-blerd-moments-from-anime-to-babymetal) — Revolt\n- [Rappers Who Like Anime: Megan Thee Stallion, Lil Uzi Vert & More](https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/674528-rappers-like-anime) — HotNewHipHop\n- [Megan Thee Stallion Stuns in Cosplay to Present Anime of the Year](https://www.complex.com/music/a/alex-ocho/megan-thee-stallion-stuns-in-cosplay-for-crunchyroll) — Complex\n- [RZA, SSGKobe, IDK, Robb Banks and Open Mike Eagle Explore Hip-Hop's Connection to Anime](https://www.xxlmag.com/hip-hop-anime/) — XXL\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-15",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-11-15"
