    "slug": "nas-beat-street-broadway-musical-schele-williams-kristoffer-diaz-mass-appeal-mamdani-bronx-school-of-hip-hop-september-30-2026",
    "pillar": "hiphop",
    "title": "Nas Takes Beat Street to Broadway and Hip-Hop Into a Bronx School",
    "headline": "Nas: Beat Street on\nBroadway, a School in the Bronx",
    "subhead": "The 1984 film gets a musical with The Wiz director Schele Williams and Hell's Kitchen playwright Kristoffer Diaz, backed by Mass Appeal and the movie's original producers. Same week, Nas sat with Mayor Mamdani at the city's first public hip-hop high school.",
    "description": "Nas is adapting Beat Street for Broadway with Schele Williams, Kristoffer Diaz and Jason Michael Webb via Mass Appeal, and sat with NYC Mayor Mamdani at the new Bronx School of Hip-Hop.",
    "keywords": [
      "Nas",
      "Beat Street",
      "Beat Street Broadway",
      "Schele Williams",
      "Kristoffer Diaz",
      "Mass Appeal",
      "Zohran Mamdani",
      "Bronx School of Hip-Hop",
      "Arthur Baker",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX",
      "Black-owned media"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "NAS: BEAT STREET ON\nBROADWAY, A BRONX SCHOOL",
    "ogAccent": "1D4ED8",
    "trendScore": 70,
    "heroCredit": "XXL / Adam Gray/Getty Images / Atlantic Records",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-beat-street-film-broadway-creative-team/",
    "heroFocus": "center",
    "heroImage": "https://townsquare.media/site/812/files/2026/09/attachment-nas-beat-street-creative-team.jpg?w=1200&format=natural",
    "body": "Two Nas stories in one week, both about who owns hip-hop's history and who teaches it.\n\n## Beat Street, the musical\nPer Deadline via [XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-beat-street-film-broadway-creative-team/), Nas has assembled the creative team to turn the 1984 film into a Broadway musical: Schele Williams, who directed The Wiz revival, will direct; Kristoffer Diaz, who wrote the book for the Tony-nominated, Grammy-winning Hell's Kitchen, writes this one; Jason Michael Webb handles music supervision, arrangements and orchestrations. The team ran a 29-hour workshop in August. Producers are Richard Fearn, Arthur Baker, Michael Holman and Peter Bittenbender with Mass Appeal, the company Nas co-founded. Baker and Holman produced the original movie. Nas has been attached since 2024: \"Beat Street wasn't just a film – it spread the revolution of hip-hop culture throughout the country and the world.\"\n\n!youtube(https://youtu.be/7bYXW4x7RXk)\n\n## Why Beat Street\nHarry Belafonte produced it, Stan Lathan directed it, and the cameos are the founding roster: Kool Herc, Bambaataa and the Soulsonic Force, Doug E. Fresh, Treacherous Three, Melle Mel on the title track. The Roxy battle scene is the one every B-boy documentary still cuts to. Hamilton proved Broadway would pay for hip-hop's vocabulary. This is hip-hop bringing its own IP to the stage, with its own producers on the sheet.\n\n## The Bronx school\nOn Wednesday, Sept. 30, Mayor Zohran Mamdani's office posted a short documentary of the mayor and Nas visiting the Bronx School of Hip-Hop, a new public high school in Claremont that opened in September and uses the culture to teach math, science, history and literature, per [XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-mayor-zohran-mamdani-bronx-school-hip-hop/). Mamdani: \"We can't be content with having been the birthplace of hip-hop. We wanted to still be the home.\" Founding principal Jason Reyes: \"It validates that our culture is great and that our culture means something.\" Nas: \"It's all about the youth and how do we get them to avoid the mistakes that so many people made.\"\n\n!youtube(https://youtu.be/0XwqsCBgZWo)\n\n![Nas and Mayor Mamdani via XXL](https://townsquare.media/site/812/files/2026/09/attachment-nyc-mayor-zohran-mamdani-nas-hip-hop.jpg?w=1200&format=natural \"XXL / NYC Mayor's Office|https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-mayor-zohran-mamdani-bronx-school-hip-hop/\")\n\n## The read\nMass Appeal already runs the catalog business (Hip Hop 50, the Legend Has It series). A Broadway title is a different kind of asset: a license that pays for decades and tours without the artist. Pair that with a public school teaching the subject, and you get the version of legacy the culture usually has to fight for after the founders are gone. Nas is doing it while the Roxy generation can still take a bow.\n\n## Sources\n- [XXL, Sept. 30, 2026: Nas takes classic Beat Street film to Broadway with new creative team](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-beat-street-film-broadway-creative-team/)\n- [XXL, Sept. 30, 2026: Nas and Mayor Zohran Mamdani discuss hip-hop's future with opening of Bronx School of Hip-Hop](https://www.xxlmag.com/nas-mayor-zohran-mamdani-bronx-school-hip-hop/)\n- [NYC Mayor's Office on YouTube, Sept. 30, 2026: Mayor and Nas sit down to discuss hip-hop in NYC](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XwqsCBgZWo)\n\nBuilt for the culture. Operated by PHRHX through Sneakz & Beatz LLC. Black-owned, four pillars: sneakers, hip-hop, anime, gaming.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-02",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-10-02"
