    "trendScore": 70,
    "heroCredit": "XXL",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.xxlmag.com/chance-the-rapper-name-change/",
    "body": "Chance the Rapper says he might drop the \"Chance.\" Not for a new name, for a plainer one: \"The Rapper.\" XXL reported it Oct. 2 off a recent episode of Raven-Symoné and Miranda Maday's *Tea Time* podcast.\n\n## What he said\nPer [XXL](https://www.xxlmag.com/chance-the-rapper-name-change/), the comments land around the 20:41 mark of the episode, which dropped Oct. 1. His reasoning is about focus. He wants the work to be the point and the personal stuff to take a back seat. The line XXL pulled: \"I really love the art form. I really love words.\"\n\nThat's the whole confirmed story. It's a man thinking out loud on a podcast. XXL's framing is that he's considering it. Nobody announced a rebrand, no paperwork, no new handle, no date. Treat it as a mood, not a move.\n\n## Why it reads as honest\nLook at the timing. Chance is out on the road celebrating ten years of *Coloring Book*, the tape that proved an independent artist could win on the biggest stage without a label deal. We got into that run in [our Coloring Book breakdown](/the-lane/chance-the-rapper-coloring-book-no-label-experiment-what-came-after-2026). A decade later, the pitch from him is almost the opposite of a personality brand. Less diary, more bars.\n\nThat's a real shift for an artist whose appeal was always how open he was. Faith, family, the whole open book. Pulling back from that and saying \"just call me the rapper\" is either a reset or a joke he'll walk back by next week. XXL doesn't say which, and neither will I.\n\n## The bigger point\nHip-hop runs on names. Names are branding, protection, and history in one word. A rapper telling you he wants the title to mean the craft, not the person, is a statement about how much of yourself the internet gets to keep. Plenty of us feel that. You don't owe anybody the whole story to be good at the job.\n\nWe'll watch whether this turns into anything on the tour or in a new project. Until then, it's one podcast answer. A good one.\n\nBuilt for the culture. Operated from San Diego. Run by PHRHX through Sneakz & Beatz LLC. Black-owned, four pillars: sneakers, hip-hop, anime, gaming.\n\n## Sources\n- [XXL, Oct. 2, 2026: Chance the Rapper Considers Name Change](https://www.xxlmag.com/chance-the-rapper-name-change/)\n",
