    "body": "NBA YoungBoy told i-D he wants to quit rap \"right now.\" XXL reported the cover story on October 6, 2026, and the headline is the easy part. The sentence after it is the real one: he feels the situation is \"way bigger\" than him.\n\n## What he actually said\n\nPer XXL's summary, he said he'd step back if he couldn't make people happy with the music, and that his legacy will show up through his children, not money or plaques. XXL is summarizing and quoting the i-D piece, so the full interview is the place to check tone and context.\n\nThis isn't a first. XXL notes that in August he told Funny Marco his career is ending soon, with \"one or two\" albums left and one more tour.\n\n## The output says one thing, the mouth says another\n\nHere's the tension. XXL notes he released *Slime Cry* in January and *ML2* in June. Then there's the *American YoungBoy* documentary, which XXL says pulled over $1 million in its first box-office week. A man ready to leave doesn't usually keep stacking projects and theaters.\n\nLook at it two ways. One reading: it's burnout, and a rapper who's put out music at a pace most labels can't plan around is telling the truth. The other: retirement talk is part of the rollout. Both can be true. Nobody outside his circle knows which, so don't pretend to.\n\n## Why it hits different\n\nYoungBoy built his lane without the usual machinery. Fans treat the catalog like a diary, and a lot of them grew up on it. When the author says the diary might close, that lands as a personal event, not a business story.\n\nWe'll believe the last tour when the dates drop. Until then, he's said it before.\n\nBuilt for the culture. Operated from San Diego. Run by PHRHX through Sneakz & Beatz LLC. Black-owned, four pillars: sneakers, hip-hop, anime, gaming.\n\n## Sources\n- [XXL, YoungBoy Never Broke Again Wants to Quit Rap Right Now, Oct. 6, 2026](https://www.xxlmag.com/youngboy-never-broke-again-quit-rap/)\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-09",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-10-09"
